{"study_accession":"PRJNA1045717","secondary_study_accession":"SRP474726","title":"Comparative transcriptomic analysis of circulating endothelial cells in sickle cell stroke","description":"Ischemic Stroke (IS) is one of the most impairing complications of sickle cell anemia (SCA), responsible for 20% of mortality in patients. Rheological alterations, adhesive properties of sickle reticulocytes, leukocyte adhesion, inflammation and endothelial dysfunction are related to the vasculopathy observed prior to ischemic events. The role of the vascular endothelium in this complex cascade of mechanisms is emphasized, as well as in the process of ischemia-induced repair and neovascularization. The aim of the present study was to perform a comparative transcriptomic analysis of endothelial colony-forming cells (ECFCs) from SCA patients with and without IS. Next, to gain further insights of the biological relevance of differentially expressed genes (DEGs), functional enrichment analysis, protein-protein interaction network (PPI) construction and in silico prediction of regulatory factors were performed. Among the 2,469 DEGs, genes related to cell proliferation (AKT1, E2F1, CDCA5, EGFL7), migration (AKT1, HRAS), angiogenesis (AKT1, EGFL7) and defense response pathways (HRAS, IRF3, TGFB1), important endothelial cell molecular mechanisms in post ischemia repair were identified. Despite the severity of IS in SCA, widely accepted molecular targets are still lacking, especially related to stroke outcome. Thus, these exploratory results may contribute to a better understanding of the role of endothelial cells in SCA ischemic stroke recovery, and promote new insights to further studies on therapeutic strategies for this severe complication. Overall design: RNA-Seq was performed from RNA samples extracted from endothelial colony-forming cells (ECFCs) obtained from eight sickle cell anemia (SCA) patients, divided into case group: four individuals with ischemic stroke (IS), and control group: four individuals without the occurrence of IS.","pubmed":"38386032","geo_series":"","n_samples":8,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN38454046","study_accession":"PRJNA1045717","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"Sample_8, C","description":"Sample_8, C","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7919570","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2024-04-09","attributes":[["source_name","ECFC, without ischemic stroke"],["tissue","peripheral blood"],["cell_type","Endothelial colony-forming cell"],["genotype","SCA without IS"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR26975839","experiment_accession":"SRX22669285","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":31280550,"base_count":6318671100,"first_public":"2024-04-09","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM7919570: Sample_8, C Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN38454047","study_accession":"PRJNA1045717","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"Sample_7, C","description":"Sample_7, C","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7919569","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2024-04-09","attributes":[["source_name","ECFC, without ischemic stroke"],["tissue","peripheral blood"],["cell_type","Endothelial colony-forming cell"],["genotype","SCA without IS"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR26975840","experiment_accession":"SRX22669284","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina HiSeq 2500","read_count":38421120,"base_count":7761066240,"first_public":"2024-04-09","experiment_title":"Illumina HiSeq 2500 sequencing: GSM7919569: Sample_7, C Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN38454048","study_accession":"PRJNA1045717","blood_class":"whole_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"Sample_6, 